        <h1>The Price of a Close Approach</h1>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="article-grid">
      <aside class="contents" aria-label="Article sections">
        <p>Contents</p>
        <ol>
          {sections.map(([id, label]) => (
            <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </aside>

      <div class="article-copy">
<p class="status"><em>Status as of 17 October 2026, 21:00 Pacific (04:00 UTC on the 18th). This story is still moving.</em></p>
        <div class="opening">

<p>At 02:17 UTC on 12 October, a Russian military satellite called Cosmos 2612 fired its engine and changed its velocity by 27.9 meters per second. The burn dropped it to about 555 kilometers, one kilometer below ICEYE-X36, a U.S.-registered radar satellite operated by the Finnish-founded company ICEYE, and put it in the same orbital plane at an inclination of 97.85 degrees. That is the account in <a href="https://isruniversity.com/2026/10/12/issue-155/">Integrity ISR's Issue 155</a>, drawn from commercial tracking providers that report to the U.S. Space Force's Joint Commercial Operations cell.</p>
<p>ICEYE-X36 weighs about 90 kilograms. Its last maneuver that anyone outside the company could detect was a small orbit adjustment in late June. Nobody outside ICEYE knows whether it has the propellant to get out of the way.</p>
<p>Cosmos 2612, by Integrity's count, has spent about 175 m/s of velocity change since launch. That is roughly 60 m/s more than any of the four sister satellites launched with it. Changing a satellite's orbital plane in low Earth orbit takes a lot of propellant, which is one reason analysts read these maneuvers as deliberate.</p>
        </div>

        <section id="from-475-km">
          <h2>1. From 475 kilometers to a few meters</h2>
<p>On 12 and 13 October the two satellites were about 475 km apart, with Cosmos 2612 trailing and closing slowly. Then it sped up. <a href="https://www.axios.com/2026/10/17/russia-ukraine-satellite-space">LeoLabs told Axios</a> that the gap was more than 200 miles on Thursday and roughly three miles on Friday afternoon. The company called it a deliberate approach, said behavior like this is relatively uncommon in low Earth orbit, and said the intent remains uncertain.</p>
          <figure class="fig sep-fig" aria-labelledby="fig-sep-title">
            <p class="fig-kicker">Figure 1</p>
            <p class="fig-title" id="fig-sep-title">Reported separation between Cosmos 2612 and ICEYE-X36</p>
            <div class="sep-chart" role="img" aria-label="Five reported separations on a log scale: about 475 kilometers on 12 to 13 October, more than 320 kilometers on 15 October, about 4.8 kilometers on the afternoon of 16 October, 1 to 2 kilometers at 18:15 UTC on 17 October, and a predicted few meters early on 18 October.">
              <div class="sep-axis" aria-hidden="true">
                <span style="--x:0%">1 m</span><span style="--x:16.67%">10 m</span><span style="--x:33.33%">100 m</span><span style="--x:50%">1 km</span><span style="--x:66.67%">10 km</span><span style="--x:83.33%">100 km</span><span style="--x:100%">1,000 km</span>
              </div>
              <ol class="sep-rows">
                <li><span class="sep-when"><b>12–13 Oct</b> Integrity ISR, from JCO commercial tracking</span><span class="sep-track"><span class="sep-dot" style="--x:94.61%" title="About 475 km, 12–13 Oct (Integrity ISR)"></span><span class="sep-val" style="--x:94.61%">~475 km</span></span></li>
                <li><span class="sep-when"><b>15 Oct</b> LeoLabs, via Axios</span><span class="sep-track"><span class="sep-dot more" style="--x:91.79%" title="More than 200 miles (320 km), 15 Oct (LeoLabs via Axios)"></span><span class="sep-val" style="--x:91.79%">&gt;320 km</span></span></li>
                <li><span class="sep-when"><b>16 Oct, afternoon</b> LeoLabs, via Axios</span><span class="sep-track"><span class="sep-dot" style="--x:61.4%" title="About 3 miles (4.8 km), 16 Oct afternoon (LeoLabs via Axios)"></span><span class="sep-val" style="--x:61.4%">~4.8 km</span></span></li>
                <li><span class="sep-when"><b>17 Oct, 18:15 UTC</b> Langbroek, public orbital elements</span><span class="sep-track"><span class="sep-range" style="--x1:50.0%;--x2:55.02%" title="1–2 km, 17 Oct 18:15 UTC (Langbroek, from TLEs)"></span><span class="sep-val" style="--x:52.93%">1–2 km</span></span></li>
                <li class="predicted"><span class="sep-when"><b>18 Oct, early UTC</b> Langbroek's prediction</span><span class="sep-track"><span class="sep-range open" style="--x1:0.0%;--x2:16.67%" title="A few meters, predicted for early 18 Oct UTC; not observed"></span><span class="sep-val" style="--x:7.95%">a few meters, predicted</span></span></li>
              </ol>
              <span class="sep-ref" style="--xr:0.6640999999999999" aria-hidden="true"><span>~10 km: usual practice, per Brynildson</span></span>
            </div>
            <figcaption>Log scale. Each row is a different source, method and time, so the points are not joined into one line. The last row is a forecast from two-line elements, which carry no uncertainty data; it has not been observed. Sources: <a href="https://isruniversity.com/2026/10/12/issue-155/">Integrity ISR</a>, <a href="https://www.axios.com/2026/10/17/russia-ukraine-satellite-space">Axios</a>, <a href="https://sattrackcam.blogspot.com/2026/10/kosmos-2612-and-iceye-x36-worrisome-cat.html">Langbroek</a>.</figcaption>
          </figure>

<p>Marco Langbroek, a Dutch analyst who has tracked military satellites from Leiden for years, was skeptical over the summer that Cosmos 2612 was after X36 at all. The orbits were similar, but the geometry did not produce frequent close passes, and he thought the match could be coincidence. The 30 September maneuvers changed his mind. In a <a href="https://sattrackcam.blogspot.com/2026/10/kosmos-2612-and-iceye-x36-worrisome-cat.html">post on Saturday</a>, he put the separation at 1 to 2 km as of 18:15 UTC, based on the latest public orbital elements, and calculated that Cosmos 2612 might pass within a few meters of X36 in the early hours of 18 October UTC.</p>
          <figure class="fig media-fig" aria-labelledby="fig-video-title">
            <p class="fig-kicker">Figure 2</p>
            <p class="fig-title" id="fig-video-title">Cosmos 2612 relative to ICEYE-X36, projected 24 hours ahead</p>
            <div class="media-frame">
              <video controls muted playsinline loop preload="metadata" width="768" height="432" aria-describedby="fig-video-cap">
                <source src="/videos/cosmos-2612-iceye-x36-cohen.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
              </video>
            </div>
            <figcaption id="fig-video-cap">ICEYE-X36 sits at the origin of a radial / in-track / cross-track (RIC) frame; the trace is Cosmos 2612. The animation is a 24-hour coast from 12:00 UTC on 17 October to 12:00 UTC on 18 October, assuming no further maneuvers, computed from public orbital data. One second of video is about 30 minutes. It shows where the orbits lead, not observed positions. Visualization by <a href="https://x.com/AdamCohen8">Adam Cohen (@AdamCohen8)</a> using Orbital Engineering Lab, from his <a href="https://x.com/AdamCohen8/status/2111477601583989226">17 October thread</a>.</figcaption>
          </figure>

<p>That pass, if it happened, would have taken place in the last few hours. As of this writing there is no public report of it, and no report of damage or lost service. Langbroek's figure is a prediction from two-line element sets, a public orbit format that NASA's conjunction guidance notes lacks the uncertainty information you need for meter-level estimates. It tells you the approach was continuing. It is not a measured distance.</p>
<p>Clayton Swope at CSIS described the Friday distance to Axios as bumper-to-bumper. Aaron Brynildson, who teaches space law at the University of Mississippi, told the same reporters that no rule sets a minimum distance between satellites. Countries usually keep around six miles apart as a matter of practice.</p>
<p>ICEYE declined to comment. The Pentagon said it had nothing to release.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="why-this-satellite">
          <h2>2. Why this satellite</h2>
<p>ICEYE signed a contract in August 2022 giving Ukraine's government access to its radar constellation, and this January it announced expanded work with a customer inside Ukraine's Ministry of Defence. Synthetic aperture radar sees through cloud and at night, which makes it useful for watching military equipment move. A Ukrainian official told Axios that the military uses ICEYE's capabilities but could not say whether X36 specifically is part of that work.</p>
<p>This is also not the first approach. Russia launched six satellites, Cosmos 2609 through 2614, from Plesetsk in April. According to <a href="https://www.csis.org/analysis/could-russia-be-poised-use-orbit-weapon-against-commercial-satellite">CSIS</a>, five of them made plane changes in May to match the ICEYE satellite's orbit, and Cosmos 2609 came within about 2.5 km of it on 26 May. Then the planes drifted apart until Cosmos 2612 went back on 30 September.</p>
<p>Possible reasons include inspection, collecting signals, testing a capability, or sending a message to the countries buying the imagery. None of these has been established, and each would mean something different for ICEYE's customers.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="the-vpn-attack">
          <h2>3. The attack that already happened came through a VPN</h2>
<p>If you want to know what conflict does to a commercial space business, the best evidence is from 24 February 2022, the morning Russia invaded Ukraine, and it involved no spacecraft maneuvers at all.</p>
<p>At about 03:02 UTC, Viasat's KA-SAT network started seeing large volumes of malicious traffic from customer modems in Ukraine. Around 04:15 UTC, modems across much of Europe began dropping offline, and over roughly 45 minutes tens of thousands of them disappeared from the network. According to <a href="https://www.viasat.com/perspectives/corporate/2022/ka-sat-network-cyber-attack-overview/">Viasat's own incident report</a>, the attacker had come in through a misconfigured VPN appliance, moved into the network segment used to manage the system, and used legitimate management commands to overwrite the flash memory on residential modems in bulk.</p>
          <figure class="fig timeline-fig" aria-labelledby="fig-kasat-title">
            <p class="fig-kicker">Figure 3</p>
            <p class="fig-title" id="fig-kasat-title">KA-SAT, 24 February 2022: no satellite touched</p>
            <ol class="timeline">
              <li><time>03:02 UTC</time><p>Large volumes of malicious traffic start coming from modems in Ukraine.</p></li>
              <li><time>~04:15 UTC</time><p>Modems across much of Europe begin dropping off the network. Tens of thousands go dark over about 45 minutes.</p></li>
              <li><time>Same day</time><p>Network largely stabilized within hours.</p></li>
              <li><time>Following days</time><p>Fully stabilized. Modems restored by over-the-air update, factory reset, or replacement.</p></li>
              <li><time>30 Mar 2022</time><p>Viasat publishes its incident overview: nearly 30,000 replacement modems shipped to distributors.</p></li>
              <li><time>10 May 2022</time><p>The EU attributes the attack to Russia.</p></li>
            </ol>
            <figcaption>Entry point: a misconfigured VPN appliance on the ground network. Sources: <a href="https://www.viasat.com/perspectives/corporate/2022/ka-sat-network-cyber-attack-overview/">Viasat</a>, <a href="https://www.consilium.europa.eu/en/press/press-releases/2022/05/10/russian-cyber-operations-against-ukraine-declaration-by-the-high-representative-on-behalf-of-the-european-union/">Council of the EU</a>.</figcaption>
          </figure>

<p>The satellite was fine. Customers in Ukraine and across Europe lost their connections anyway. Viasat stabilized the network within hours, fully within days, and shipped nearly 30,000 replacement modems to distributors. In May 2022 the <a href="https://www.consilium.europa.eu/en/press/press-releases/2022/05/10/russian-cyber-operations-against-ukraine-declaration-by-the-high-representative-on-behalf-of-the-european-union/">European Union attributed the attack to Russia</a>.</p>
<p>For an operator or investor, KA-SAT is the best-documented case of what conflict costs a commercial space company. The damage was done on the ground. Most of the people who lost service had nothing to do with the war, and the company recovered by buying and shipping hardware.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="where-the-money-sits">
          <h2>4. Where the money actually sits</h2>
<p>Three numbers get passed around in space-economy coverage, and they measure different things.</p>
