<p>So the largest single slice of the satellite industry's revenue is receivers on Earth that depend on a signal from orbit. That signal can be jammed from the ground without anyone touching a satellite.</p>
<p>A 2019 study presented to the U.S. GPS advisory board <a href="https://www.gps.gov/governance/advisory/meetings/2019-11/gallaher.pdf">modeled a 30-day GPS outage</a> at more than $1 billion a day in U.S. losses, about $30.3 billion in total. Maritime operations accounted for $10.4 billion of that, mostly ports, and telecommunications for $9.8 billion. It is an old, conditional scenario. It still gives a sense of how much ordinary commerce runs on timing and position data.</p>
<p>The imaging business works the other way. Remote sensing revenue is small, but governments now buy it in wartime, which raises demand and exposure together.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="before-anything-breaks">
          <h2>5. What it costs before anything breaks</h2>
<p>Nobody has published what the last three weeks have cost ICEYE, and the company has said nothing. We can list the kinds of cost an operator takes on when another country's satellite parks next to theirs, without assigning numbers to them.</p>
<p>Someone has to watch it around the clock. If the operator decides to move, it spends propellant that was budgeted for the satellite's working life, and a 90 kg satellite does not carry much. Imaging tasks may be dropped while the spacecraft maneuvers. Customers, especially government customers, will ask what is happening and what the operator is doing about it. If the operator carries insurance, the policy wording on hostile acts, war and cyber events decides what is recoverable, and those exclusions vary.</p>
<p>Then there is financing. A company raising money for a constellation now has to answer questions about a risk that was abstract a year ago. We do not know yet whether any insurer or lender has changed terms because of this encounter. We would like to.</p>
<p>If you operate satellites, or work in space insurance or finance and have seen this come up, we want to hear what a close approach actually costs you in staff hours, fuel, lost collections or changed terms. Send it to <a href="mailto:info@populationmars.com">info@populationmars.com</a>. Real numbers will go in a follow-up piece, with your name or without it.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="the-us-does-this-too">
          <h2>6. The United States does this too</h2>
<p>A fair reading of Cosmos 2612 needs the same standard applied to everyone.</p>
<p>The U.S. Space Force operates the <a href="https://www.ussf-cfc.spaceforce.mil/About-Us/Fact-Sheets/Display/Article/2381739/geosynchronous-space-situational-awareness-program">Geosynchronous Space Situational Awareness Program</a>, satellites built to approach and inspect other objects in geostationary orbit, and the Secure World Foundation has documented U.S. approaches to foreign satellites. In September 1985 the U.S. Air Force destroyed its own Solwind satellite with a missile fired from an F-15. China destroyed one of its weather satellites in 2007. Russia's November 2021 missile test against its own Cosmos 1408 produced more than 1,500 trackable fragments, according to U.S. Space Command.</p>
<p>On 14 September, Air Force Secretary Troy Meink told the Air &amp; Space Forces Association's conference in National Harbor, Maryland, that the Space Force now has <a href="https://www.airandspaceforces.com/meink-space-force-on-orbit-space-control-weapons/">"on-orbit space control weapons."</a> He would not say whether they are kinetic or non-kinetic, or whether they have been tested, and he said the wording was deliberate. That is a public acknowledgment of weapons already in orbit. It does not tell us when they were deployed, how many there are, or what they do.</p>
<p>The law gives little guidance on how close is too close. The Outer Space Treaty bans nuclear weapons in orbit and national claims of territory, and requires "due regard" for other countries' activities. It sets no keep-out distance. The Liability Convention makes a state liable for damage in space only where fault can be shown, and showing fault depends on evidence that is often classified or does not exist.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="the-navy-comparison">
          <h2>7. The navy comparison, with dates</h2>
<p>A common argument holds that American naval power kept sea lanes open after 1945, trade grew, and space needs the same thing. Naval power was part of it. So were the Bretton Woods monetary agreements of 1944 and the trade rules that became GATT in 1947, along with reconstruction lending and new shipping technology.</p>
<p>The Strait of Hormuz this month shows the limit of the military half on its own. The EIA's <a href="https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/archives/oct26.pdf">October 2026 Short-Term Energy Outlook</a> describes constrained flows, convoys and rerouting, in a region where the U.S. Navy has operated for decades.</p>
<p>Orbit is also harder to patrol than a strait. Every satellite is moving, changing orbit costs fuel and time, debris from a fight stays in the way of everyone afterward, and the most damaging attack so far went through a ground network.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="the-missing-product">
          <h2>8. The missing product is evidence</h2>
<p>Langbroek added a note to his Saturday post that is the most important sentence in this story for the industry. If ICEYE-X36 failed tonight, he wrote, it would look suspicious, but it would be very hard to attribute the failure to Cosmos 2612 without ambiguity.</p>
<p>Satellites fail on their own every year from radiation damage and software faults. An operator that loses a spacecraft next to a foreign military satellite has to show its insurer and customers what happened, and possibly a court. Today that evidence is scattered across classified sensors, commercial trackers like LeoLabs and Slingshot, public orbital elements, and the operator's own telemetry, and none of it is collected in a form designed to settle a dispute.</p>
<p>That is a real business gap. Tracking companies already sell conjunction alerts. The next step is evidence an insurer or a government will accept: maneuver records with stated uncertainty, timestamps that can be checked independently, and a clear record of who consented to what. Servicing and debris-removal companies face the same question from the other side. A spacecraft that can approach and grab a dead satellite can also approach a live one, so Astroscale or ClearSpace will need to prove what their vehicles did and did not do.</p>
<p>The same problem will follow industry out of Earth orbit. NASA's lunar relay contract with Intuitive Machines, announced in September 2024, has a <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-selects-lunar-relay-contractor-for-near-space-network-services/">ceiling of $4.82 billion</a> over up to ten years. That is a maximum, not money spent. It is also a shared communications and navigation service that every lunar mission will lean on, and the rules for who can approach it, and how a failure gets investigated, have not been written.</p>
